{"causes": ["The established dynastic power of the Uí Néill in northern Ireland provided the framework for Eochaid's ascent to kingship.", "His direct lineage from powerful High Kings like Muirchertach mac Muiredaig granted him significant legitimacy and a strong claim to authority within the Cenél nEógain.", "The ongoing competition among various Uí Néill branches for suzerainty over regional kingdoms propelled individuals like Eochaid into positions of leadership."], "effects": ["Eochaid's reign contributed to the consolidation of Cenél nEógain power in the kingdom of Ailech during the mid-6th century.", "His inclusion in some early Irish king-lists as a High King influences modern historical interpretations of early Irish political succession and Uí Néill dominance.", "His death in 571 CE likely triggered further dynastic struggles among his kinsmen for control of Ailech and potentially the High Kingship."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "cenel-neogain", "sourceName": "Cenél nEógain", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill", "targetName": "Eochaid mac Domnaill", "context": "Eochaid mac Domnaill was a member of the powerful Cenél nEógain branch of the Uí Néill dynasty."}, {"sourceSlug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill", "sourceName": "Eochaid mac Domnaill", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "kingdom-of-ailech", "targetName": "Kingdom of Ailech", "context": "Eochaid mac Domnaill ruled as king in Ailech from 565 CE to 571 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "muirchertach-mac-muiredaig", "sourceName": "Muirchertach mac Muiredaig", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill", "targetName": "Eochaid mac Domnaill", "context": "Eochaid mac Domnaill's political legitimacy and claim to power were significantly influenced by being the grandson of the powerful High King Muirchertach mac Muiredaig."}, {"sourceSlug": "domnall-ilchelgach", "sourceName": "Domnall Ilchelgach", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill", "targetName": "Eochaid mac Domnaill", "context": "Eochaid mac Domnaill succeeded his father, Domnall Ilchelgach, in the regional kingship of Ailech."}, {"sourceSlug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill", "sourceName": "Eochaid mac Domnaill", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "early-medieval-irish-kingship", "targetName": "Early Medieval Irish Kingship", "context": "Eochaid mac Domnaill actively participated in the system of early medieval Irish kingship, holding regional power and potentially the High Kingship."}], "places": [{"name": "County Donegal, Ireland", "role": "Center of Ailech"}, {"name": "County Londonderry, Northern Ireland", "role": "Part of Ailech"}, {"name": "Ireland", "role": "Kingdom's location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Eochaid mac Domnaill", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "eochaid-mac-domnaill"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1260251", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eochaid_mac_Domnaill"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Eochaid mac Domnaill held significant regional power as king of Ailech and is listed in some accounts as a High King of Ireland, contributing to the Uí Néill's consolidation of power in the North that shaped Irish politics for centuries. His reign provides crucial insight into the complex dynastic struggles and succession patterns of early medieval Ireland.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-20T23:36:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44834533979", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Eochaid mac Domnaill, also Eochaid Find, was an Irish king who is included in some lists as a High King of Ireland. He was the son of Domnall Ilchelgach and grandson of Muirchertach mac Muiredaig, also considered high kings. He was a member of the Cenél nEógain branch of the northern Uí Néill. He ruled in Ailech from 565 to 571.", "newValue": "Eochaid mac Domnaill, also known as Eochaid Find, was a prominent Irish king of the northern Uí Néill dynasty who ruled the kingdom of Ailech from 565 CE until his death in 571 CE. His position within the powerful Cenél nEógain branch, descended from the legendary Niall Noígíallach, placed him at the heart of early medieval Irish dynastic struggles, where he is occasionally listed among the High Kings of Ireland. His reign occurred during a transformative period in Ireland, as the influence of Christianity solidified and regional kingdoms vied for suzerainty, shaping the political landscape of the island for centuries.

Son of Domnall Ilchelgach and grandson of the esteemed Muirchertach mac Muiredaig, both of whom are also counted among the High Kings, Eochaid inherited a legacy of significant political power and ambition. His brief six-year rule over Ailech, a kingdom centered in modern-day County Donegal and Derry, saw him continue the expansionist policies typical of the Uí Néill. Though specific military campaigns or legislative acts from his personal reign are sparsely documented in contemporary annals, his inclusion in some regnal lists as High King suggests he achieved a degree of dominance, possibly through alliances or military successes against rival Uí Néill factions or neighboring Ulster kingdoms.

Eochaid's death in 571 CE marked a transition within the Cenél nEógain, leading to further dynastic competition among his kinsmen. While his direct impact on the broader course of Irish history is less pronounced than some of his more famous ancestors or successors, his reign contributed to the consolidation of Uí Néill power in the North, laying groundwork for the later prominence of the Cenél nEógain, who would dominate the High Kingship for much of the 7th and 8th centuries. His place in the king-lists provides crucial data for modern historians reconstructing the complex and often contested succession of early Irish monarchs."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T23:36:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44834533979", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The established dynastic power of the Uí Néill in northern Ireland provided the framework for Eochaid's ascent to kingship.", "His direct lineage from powerful High Kings like Muirchertach mac Muiredaig granted him significant legitimacy and a strong claim to authority within the Cenél nEógain.", "The ongoing competition among various Uí Néill branches for suzerainty over regional kingdoms propelled individuals like Eochaid into positions of leadership."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T23:36:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44834533979", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "571 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "571 CE"}], "newValue": ["Eochaid's reign contributed to the consolidation of Cenél nEógain power in the kingdom of Ai